  "course_description": "Offerings vary each semester.",
  "course_notes": "\"Bad\" Mothers, Defiant Daughters: This course takes as its jumping-off point the often fraught relationships between mothers and daughters. What makes for a \"good\" mother or daughter? A bad one? What do intergenerational feminine intimacies, and their supposed \"failures,\" teach us about the politics of mothering? How do ideologies of race, class, gender, sexuality, and ability work to create these definitions, and what are their material effects? Exploring experiences of mothering and daughter-ing through historical, (trans)cultural, literary, and activist texts, students will explore how mothers and daughters reinforce and disrupt dominant ideologies of care, responsibility and kinship. This course will draw on feminist, queer, critical race and decolonial frameworks.",
